The linker errors indicate that the device driver instances for the DAC emulators are not being created. This is typically caused by an issue in the device tree overlay file where the nodes are not correctly identified as instances of a driver.

The nodes `dac-emulator-0`, `dac-emulator-1`, and `dac-emulator-many` do not follow the standard `name@unit-address` convention for instantiable devices in Zephyr's device tree. The build system relies on this convention to number and instantiate devices for a given driver compatible.

The fix is to rename the nodes in `tests/drivers/dac/dac_emul/boards/native_sim.overlay` to use the `name@unit-address` format (e.g., `dac-emulator@0`). This allows the build system to correctly generate the device structures, which will resolve the `undefined reference` linker errors. The C code uses device tree labels (`dac_emul0`, etc.) to refer to these nodes, so no changes are needed in the source files.

